  const dText = Math.abs(inflation) &lt; 0.005 ? "the same as it would be if both conditions had the control condition's &lt;em&gt;SD&lt;/em&gt;"
    : `${inflation &gt; 0 ? "larger" : "smaller"}${d &lt; 0 ? " in absolute size" : ""} than the &lt;em&gt;d&lt;/em&gt; of ${fmt(dEqual)} that the same mean difference would give if both conditions had the control condition's &lt;em&gt;SD&lt;/em&gt;`
  const [kind, message] = valid
    ? [Math.abs(inflation) &lt; 0.005 ? "ok" : "warn", `${smdUseSmd ? `An SMD of ${fmt(target)} requires a mean difference of ${fmt(smdDiff)} scale points. ` : ""}With the same precision in both conditions, ${sdText}. Cohen's &lt;em&gt;d&lt;/em&gt; is &lt;strong&gt;${fmt(d)}&lt;/strong&gt;, ${dText}.`]
    : !reachable ? ["bad", `&lt;strong&gt;Not possible.&lt;/strong&gt; With a control mean of ${smdMean.toFixed(2)} and a precision of ${phi.toFixed(phi &lt; 1 ? 2 : 1)} on a ${lo} to ${hi} scale, the control &lt;em&gt;SD&lt;/em&gt; is ${fmt(control.sd)}, and the largest SMD ${target &gt;= 0 ? "above" : "below"} the control mean is ${fmt(Math.sign(target) * maxSmd)}. Even that requires every intervention score to be exactly ${target &gt;= 0 ? hi : lo}, so that the intervention &lt;em&gt;SD&lt;/em&gt; is zero.`]
    : ["bad", `&lt;strong&gt;Not possible.&lt;/strong&gt; A control mean of ${smdMean.toFixed(2)} and a mean difference of ${fmt(smdDiff)} put the intervention mean at ${fmt(intervention.mean)}, which is outside the ${lo} to ${hi} scale.`]
  const feedback = html`&lt;div class="sb-feedback sb-feedback-${kind}"&gt;`
  feedback.innerHTML = message
  // clicking anywhere in the right plot sets the control mean, and the precision whose curve passes through the
  // clicked SD at that mean, as in the plot above. the mean difference is unchanged
  clickToSet(right, {width: panelWidth, height: panelHeight, marginLeft: 50, marginRight: 20, marginTop: 24, marginBottom: 45, xDomain: [lo, hi], yDomain: [0, ySpan]}, (m, s) =&gt; {
    const mean = snapToInput(viewof smdMean, m), u = (mean - lo) / range
    const target = s &gt; 0 ? u * (1 - u) / (s / range) ** 2 - 1 : Infinity
    setInput(viewof smdMean, mean)
    setInput(viewof smdPhi, Math.min(100, Math.max(0.01, Math.round(target * 100) / 100)))
  })
  return html`&lt;div class="bd-panels"&gt;&lt;div&gt;${left}${readout}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;${right}${legend}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;${feedback}`
}






On a 1–7 scale with a precision of 5, a control mean of 4 and a mean difference of 2, the control condition’s SD is 1.22 and the intervention condition’s is 0.91. Cohen’s d is 1.85, compared with the 1.63 that the same two-point difference would give if both conditions had an SD of 1.22. Nothing about the spread of the data differs between the conditions: they have the same precision, so they use the same share of the largest possible variance at their means. The intervention condition’s SD is smaller only because its mean is closer to the top of the scale, and the smaller SD makes the same mean difference look larger in SD units. Increase the mean difference and the gap between the two values of d widens, because the intervention condition’s SD keeps shrinking as its mean approaches the top of the scale. Now set the control mean to 2.5 and the mean difference to 3, so that the two conditions sit symmetrically either side of the middle of the scale: their SDs are equal and there is no inflation. Whether a bounded scale inflates or deflates d depends on where the two means sit relative to the middle and the ends of the scale, not only on how far apart they are.

9.1 Influence of scale bounds on SMDs
Effect sizes can be checked in two ways. Possibility checks ask whether a reported effect size could be correct at all. Most are a form of bounds test (Chapter 13): a correlation must lie between −1 and 1, an R2 or an η2 between 0 and 1, and an odds ratio must be above 0, so a value outside these bounds cannot be correct, whatever the study. Section 13.4 lists the bounds of many common effect sizes and other statistics. Plausibility checks ask whether an effect size that is possible is believable, given what is known about the measures, the design, and the size of effects in the literature. A Cohen’s d of 5 is not impossible, but it is very unusual for most psychological interventions, and it is worth asking how it arose. Plausibility checks are the topic of this chapter, with a focus on Standardized Mean Differences (SMDs) such as Cohen’s d.
The plot above assumes normal distributions, which have no minimum or maximum. Real scales do, and this limits which effect sizes are possible. On a bounded scale, the closer a mean is to the end of the scale, the smaller its SD has to be. A large Cohen’s d requires two means that are many SDs apart, but on a bounded scale the means can only move so far apart before one of them is too close to the end of the scale to have the SD that was reported.
The plot below shows this. Set the logical minimum and maximum of the scale, the SD of both conditions in scale units, and Cohen’s d. The two conditions start at the middle of the scale and move apart as d increases, so that their means are always d × SD scale points apart. Each of the two buttons switches between two settings, and is labelled with the setting that clicking it will change to. The first chooses how the conditions move apart: at first the control condition stays in the middle of the scale and only the intervention condition moves, and the alternative is that both conditions move away from the middle by the same amount. The second chooses the direction of the effect: at first the intervention increases scores, so that Cohen’s d is positive, and the alternative is that it decreases them, so that d is negative. Each condition is drawn as a beta distribution, rescaled to the range of the scale, whose parameters are calculated from its mean and SD by the method of moments. The message under the plot says whether the combination you have chosen is possible.
