11.2.1.1 Simple calculator for effect size plausibility
Many articles, particularly experiments and RCTs, report each group’s mean, SD, and sample size. Some explicitly report a Standardized Mean Difference (aka Cohen’s d). Like the recalculation of p-values, this provides information that is repeated and often overlooked. Either the SMD can be recalculated from the summary statistics (N/M/SD) and checked for consistency or, if not reported at all, it can be checked for plausibility (Chapter 9).
Calculation of SMD effect sizes can be done the same way as meta-analysts do, e.g., using R packages that implement the formula for SMD. For example, the escalc() function from the {metafor} R package (Viechtbauer, 2010) calculates many different forms of effect size and their sampling variances from summary statistics. Here we are only considering SMD.
