11.2.2 ORs from cell counts
Many results take the form of a 2 × 2 table of counts: the number of participants in each of two groups who did and did not have some outcome. Baseline tables report them for binary characteristics, such as the number of women in each group, and trials report them for adverse events, such as the number of participants in each group who were hospitalised. From the four counts, the odds ratio (OR), its confidence interval, and the p-value of a test of the association between group and outcome can all be recalculated. As with the SMD, there is more than one defensible way to do each. The OR can be the sample OR (the product of the counts on one diagonal divided by the product of those on the other) or the conditional maximum likelihood estimate. Its confidence interval can be a Wald interval on the log scale, an exact interval, a mid-p exact interval, or a profile likelihood interval. The p-value can come from Pearson’s χ² test, with or without Yates’ continuity correction, a likelihood ratio test, or Fisher’s exact test, among others. And when one of the counts is zero, the sample OR and its Wald interval cannot be calculated unless a small number, usually 0.5, is first added to every cell.
This matters most for results that an article’s authors would rather not emphasise. Articles commonly test and report their intended benefits in detail, and describe harms only as counts, without an effect size or a test. A conclusion that a treatment is safe or well tolerated can then be checked by recalculating what the counts imply. Palhano-Fontes et al. (2019) concluded that their trial of ayahuasca for treatment-resistant depression “brings new evidence supporting the safety and therapeutic value of psychedelics”, but 4 of the 15 participants in the ayahuasca group were hospitalised, compared with none of the 15 in the placebo group (Elk & Fried, 2023). Strawn et al. (2023) concluded that escitalopram “reduced anxiety symptoms and was well tolerated” in children and adolescents with generalised anxiety disorder, but 13 of the 136 participants in the escitalopram group reported suicidal ideation, compared with 2 of the 137 in the placebo group (Plöderl et al., 2023).
The calculator below recalculates the OR, its 95% CI, and the p-value from a 2 × 2 table, using the recalc_oddsratio() function from {recalc} in your browser, loaded in the same way as in the other calculators in this chapter. Enter the four counts, and, optionally, whichever of the OR, its CI, and the p-value the article reports, to check whether they are consistent with the counts. A reported OR is also accepted if it is the reciprocal of the recalculated one, that is, if the article took the other group as the reference. The two examples load the counts above.

viewof orInput = {
  const examples = {
    "Ayahuasca: hospitalisations": {groups: ["Ayahuasca", "Placebo"], outcome: "Hospitalised", cells: [["4", "11"], ["0", "15"]]},
    "Escitalopram: suicidal ideation": {groups: ["Escitalopram", "Placebo"], outcome: "Suicidal ideation", cells: [["13", "123"], ["2", "135"]]}
  }
  const field = (label, numeric = true) =&gt; html`&lt;input type="text" class="form-control form-control-sm" inputmode="${numeric ? "numeric" : "text"}" aria-label="${label}"&gt;`
  const names = [field("Name of group 1", false), field("Name of group 2", false)]
  const cells = [0, 1].map(i =&gt; [field(`Group ${i + 1} with the event`), field(`Group ${i + 1} without the event`)])
  const outcome = html`&lt;span class="or-outcome"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;`
  const pOp = html`&lt;select class="form-select form-select-sm tt-op" aria-label="How the p-value was reported"&gt;
    &lt;option value="equals"&gt;=&lt;/option&gt;&lt;option value="less_than"&gt;&lt;&lt;/option&gt;&lt;option value="greater_than"&gt;&gt;&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;/select&gt;`
  const p = field("Reported p-value"), or = field("Reported odds ratio"), lower = field("Lower bound of the reported 95% CI"), upper = field("Upper bound of the reported 95% CI")
  for (const input of [p, or, lower, upper]) input.inputMode = "decimal"
  const load = ex =&gt; {
    ex.groups.forEach((name, i) =&gt; names[i].value = name)
    ex.cells.forEach((row, i) =&gt; row.forEach((v, j) =&gt; cells[i][j].value = v))
    outcome.textContent = ex.outcome
    for (const input of [p, or, lower, upper]) input.value = ""
  }
  load(Object.values(examples)[0])
  const exampleButtons = Object.entries(examples).map(([name, ex]) =&gt; {
    const button = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto"&gt;${name}&lt;/button&gt;`
    button.onclick = () =&gt; load(ex)
    return button
  })
  const clear = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto"&gt;Clear all&lt;/button&gt;`
  const run = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto"&gt;Check&lt;/button&gt;`
  clear.onclick = () =&gt; {
    for (const input of [...names, ...cells.flat(), p, or, lower, upper]) input.value = ""
    outcome.textContent = ""
    names[0].focus()
  }
  const view = html`&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;div class="sb-buttons sc-examples smd-examples"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Examples:&lt;/span&gt;${exampleButtons}&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="table-responsive"&gt;&lt;table class="table table-sm smd-grid or-grid"&gt;
      &lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Group&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;With the event${html`&lt;br&gt;`}${outcome}&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Without the event&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;
      &lt;tbody&gt;${[0, 1].map(i =&gt; html`&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;${names[i]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${cells[i][0]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${cells[i][1]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;`)}&lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="tt-reported"&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Reported OR (optional)&lt;/span&gt;${or}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;95% CI lower (optional)&lt;/span&gt;${lower}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;95% CI upper (optional)&lt;/span&gt;${upper}&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;&lt;span&gt;Reported &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; (optional)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="tt-pair"&gt;${pOp}${p}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="sb-buttons"&gt;${clear}${run}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`
  // the value only changes when the check button is clicked
  view.value = {runs: 0}
  run.onclick = () =&gt; {
    view.value = {
      runs: view.value.runs + 1,
      names: names.map(n =&gt; n.value.trim()), cells: cells.map(row =&gt; row.map(c =&gt; c.value.trim())),
      pOp: pOp.value, p: p.value.trim(), or: or.value.trim(), lower: lower.value.trim(), upper: upper.value.trim()
    }
    view.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", {bubbles: true}))
  }
  return view
}







