roundingRules = rounding =&gt; ({up_or_down: ["up", "down"], up_or_down_trunc: ["up", "down", "trunc"]}[rounding] ?? [rounding])
function reportedSteps(num, den, rule) {
  // a non-negative exact value num / den, in reporting steps, as it would be reported under one rule. whole-number
  // arithmetic, so that exact halves stay exact
  const q = (num - (num % den)) / den, twice = 2 * (num % den)
  return rule === "trunc" ? q
    : twice &gt; den ? q + 1 : twice &lt; den ? q
    : rule === "up" ? q + 1 : rule === "down" ? q : q % 2 === 0 ? q : q + 1
}
function grimmerSums(i, nk, scale, rounding) {
  // the whole-number sums whose mean, S / nk, is reported as i steps under any rule of the rounding option
  const rules = roundingRules(rounding), sums = []
  for (let S = Math.max(0, Math.floor((i - 1) * nk / scale)); S &lt;= Math.ceil((i + 1) * nk / scale); S++) {
    if (rules.some(rule =&gt; reportedSteps(S * scale, nk, rule) === i)) sums.push(S)
  }
  return sums
}
function grimmerSdFits(S, j, n, k, scale, rounding) {
  // can a whole-number sum of squared participant totals, Q, with the parity of S, give a sample SD reported as j steps?
  // each rule gives the interval of exact SDs (in steps) that are reported as j, and whether each end is included
  return roundingRules(rounding).some(rule =&gt; {
    let [lo, hi, loIn, hiIn] = rule === "trunc" ? [j, j + 1, true, false]
      : rule === "up" ? [j - 0.5, j + 0.5, true, false]
      : rule === "down" ? [j - 0.5, j + 0.5, false, true]
      : j % 2 === 0 ? [j - 0.5, j + 0.5, true, true] : [j - 0.5, j + 0.5, false, false]
    if (lo &lt; 0) [lo, loIn] = [0, true]
    const base = S * S / n, lower = k * k * (n - 1) * (lo / scale) ** 2 + base, upper = k * k * (n - 1) * (hi / scale) ** 2 + base
    const eps = 1e-9 * Math.max(1, upper)
    let q = loIn ? Math.ceil(lower - eps) : Math.floor(lower + eps) + 1
    if (Math.abs(q - S) % 2 === 1) q += 1
    return hiIn ? q &lt;= upper + eps : q &lt; upper - eps
  })
}































Figure 12.4: Pass rates for GRIMMER by sample size, for the chosen test, number of decimals, items averaged, and rounding rule. Each coloured line is the share of SDs within 0.25 of the stated value that pass. With the SD test only, the mean is taken to be correct, so the sum of the scores is known. With both tests, each pair of a mean and an SD must pass both. The dashed line is GRIM alone.



The SD test on its own has a simple reading. For two-decimal reporting, the share of SD values that pass is close to N × SD / 100. With an SD near 1, the SD check is about as demanding as GRIM is for the mean. With an SD near 0.5, it is twice as demanding and still rejects some values at N = 300, well past the point where GRIM gives up. With an SD near 2 or 3, it passes everything from about N = 55 or N = 35.
The two tests together show what this means for the pair. For small samples the two checks multiply: at N = 30 and an SD near 1, about 30% of means pass GRIM but under 10% of mean and SD pairs pass both. GRIMMER is therefore a much sharper test than GRIM for small samples, and the only one of the two that can still fail above N = 100, provided the SD is small. Small SDs on whole-number data are common where responses pile up at one end of a scale, which is also where the bounds methods of the next chapter are most useful.

12.6 GRIM and GRIMMER checker
An assessment rarely involves a single mean. A baseline table can contain dozens of means, SDs, and percentages, each with its own sample size. The app below runs GRIM and GRIMMER on all of them at once. It runs the {scrutiny} R package itself, the same functions used throughout this chapter, inside your browser, using webR as in the statcheck app (Section 11.1.1.1). Nothing you enter is sent anywhere. The first run takes several seconds, because R and {scrutiny} have to be downloaded, and later runs are almost instant. Its inputs follow the first tab of the INSPECT-SR consistency tester by Lukas Jung and Ian Hussey (source code), which also runs bounds checks (Chapter 13).
Enter one row per value. Each row needs:

Type. Choose Mean of whole numbers only if every value in the raw data was a whole number, such as answers on a 1–7 scale, counts, or age in years (Section 12.2.1). Choose % from yes/no count only if the percentage is a count divided by the sample size you enter, such as 10 of 22 people = 45.5%, and not a weighted percentage or an average of percentages. Otherwise, or if unsure, choose Not whole numbers, or unsure, which is the default for new rows: the row is then not tested.
Mean or percentage, and optionally the SD, exactly as printed. The number of decimals is taken from what you type, so type 4.70 rather than 4.7 if the article reported two decimals. GRIMMER only runs when an SD is given, and does not apply to percentages.
Sample size: the N behind this particular value, which may not be the total sample size (Table 12.3).
Items averaged over: the number of items averaged to make each participant’s score. It is 1 for single items, for whole-number variables such as age, and for sum scores, however many items were summed (Section 12.2.1). Set it to more than 1 only when the scale has multiple items and was mean scored.

GRIM is reported as Uninformative when it could not have failed: once N × items averaged reaches 10d, where d is the number of reported decimals, every value is attainable and every mean passes (Section 12.2.3). A row is only uninformative overall if GRIMMER was not run as well, because GRIMMER can still fail for samples where GRIM cannot (Section 12.3). Tests that could not be run on a row, because its type is Not whole numbers, or unsure, or because no mean or SD was given, are reported as Not run.
A second table summarises all the GRIM tests together, using {scrutiny}’s audit() function. It gives the number of means that are GRIM-testable, that is, for which GRIM could have failed. It gives the empirical inconsistency rate, the share of the tested means that fail GRIM, and the expected inconsistency rate, the average probability that a mean made of random digits would fail GRIM, given its sample size, items averaged, and number of decimals (Section 12.2.2). The last row is the ratio of the two. A ratio well below 1 is what we expect from accurately reported values, which fail less often than random ones. A ratio near or above 1 means the means fail about as often as random numbers would, or more often. Both rates include every tested mean, including those for which GRIM is uninformative, which count as consistent and have an expected rate of zero.
Rows can also be pasted from a spreadsheet: copy a block of cells and paste it into the cell where the block should start. The Examples button loads the examples used in this chapter, and the rounding setting chooses which rounding practices are allowed for (Note 12.1). Rows that fail are a reason to check the extraction and the assumptions in Table 12.3, not a finding in themselves.

