13.2 SD bounds
On a bounded scale, the mean and the SD are not free to vary independently of one another (Section 7.2). The scale’s minimum and maximum, the sample size, and the mean together set an upper bound on the SD, and once the data are known to be whole numbers, or to include a particular minimum and maximum, they set a lower bound too. A reported SD outside these bounds cannot be correct. Hussey et al. (2026) call this check BRIMMER, for Bounds-Related Inconsistency of Means and Errors Reported, by analogy with GRIMMER (Section 12.3). Its counterpart for the mean alone, BRIM, asks whether the reported mean can be reached at all, as in Section 13.1.
The bounds come from a series of nested constraints, each of which can only tighten them (Hussey et al., 2026):

The scale limits. The most spread out any set of scores can be is half at the minimum and half at the maximum. On a scale with range R, the sample SD can never exceed R / √2, which is reached by a sample of two people, one at each end.
The sample size. With more people, the largest possible SD falls towards R / 2. For an even sample size it is (R / 2) × √(n / (n − 1)), and for an odd one slightly less, because the people cannot be split evenly between the two ends (Popoviciu, 1935). On a 1–7 scale with 30 people, no SD can exceed 3.05, whatever the mean.
The mean. The further the mean is from the middle of the scale, the smaller the largest possible SD, which is √(n / (n − 1) × (max − M)(M − min)). This is the Bhatia–Davis bound of Section 7.2, which Muilwijk (1966) had already derived (Bhatia & Davis, 2000). It can be sharpened slightly, because the number of people at each end of the scale must be a whole number. On a 1–7 scale with 30 people and a mean of 2.97, the SD can be at most 2.84.
Integer data. Up to this point, the smallest possible SD is zero: everyone could have the same score. But when every response is a whole number, a mean with a fractional part, such as 2.97, cannot be produced by identical scores, so the SD must be above zero. This floor is usually small: 0.14 in the example.
Internal consistency. For a score made up of several items, a reported Cronbach’s α limits how much the items can disagree with one another, which lowers the ceiling and, for whole-number items, raises the floor (Hussey et al., 2026).

The scale limits are not the only minimum and maximum that constrain the SD. Many articles report the smallest and largest scores actually observed, in a table of descriptive statistics or in the text, and these say more than the scale limits do. The scale limits only say that no score lies outside them, whereas an observed minimum and maximum say that at least one person had each of those scores. A sample that includes both a low and a high score cannot have a very small SD, so the observed minimum and maximum raise the floor substantially, especially together with the mean and integer data. They also narrow the range of means that are possible at all, and they lower the ceiling, because the observed range is usually narrower than the scale’s. In the example above, if the article also reported that scores ranged from 1 to 6, the SD could be no smaller than 0.71 and no larger than 2.46.
When checking reported values, the bounds have to take rounding into account: a mean reported as 2.97 could have been anything from 2.965 to 2.975, and the bounds are calculated over that whole interval. Like GRIM and GRIMMER, BRIMMER is a necessary condition rather than a sufficient one. A reported SD outside the bounds is impossible, but one inside them may still not correspond to any real set of whole-number scores. How the bounds combine with the granularity checks, and a further, exact test, BRIMMEST, which settles whether any such set of scores exists, are the subject of Chapter 14.
Put the two constraints together, and the mean and the SD carry information about one another. When the scores are whole numbers and the scale has a minimum and a maximum, knowing the mean limits what the SD can be, and knowing the SD limits what the mean can be. Together, they also limit the distributions of scores that could have produced them: a large SD means that the scores must be piled up near the ends of the scale, and a small one means that they must be bunched together, and in a small sample a mean and SD can leave only one or a few sets of scores that produce them. Neither constraint is enough on its own. Continuous data on a bounded scale can take any SD between zero and the ceiling, and whole-number data without a minimum or maximum are subject only to the granularity constraints of Chapter 12, which say nothing about how large the SD can be.
The plot below shows this in the simplest case: a single item answered in whole numbers, with a small sample on a short scale, so that every set of scores that could have been observed can be listed. Each dot on the left is a combination of mean and SD that at least one set of scores produces, and the shaded band runs from the smallest to the largest possible SD at each mean, from the bounds above. The dotted curve is the uniform-spread bound from Section 7.2: above it, the scores are more spread out than if they were spread evenly across the whole scale. Click or drag in the plot to choose a combination: the choice snaps to the nearest dot, and the plot on the right shows the frequencies of a set of scores that produces it. When more than one set does, Show another steps through them.

viewof ssMin = Inputs.number({value: 1, step: 1, label: "Scale minimum"})
viewof ssMax = Inputs.number({value: 5, step: 1, label: "Scale maximum"})
viewof ssN = Inputs.range([2, 12], {value: 8, step: 1, label: "Sample size (n)"})























ssSets = {
  if (!(Number.isInteger(ssMin) && Number.isInteger(ssMax) && ssMax &gt; ssMin && ssMax - ssMin &lt;= 6)) return null
  const k = ssMax - ssMin + 1, n = ssN, groups = new Map(), counts = new Array(k).fill(0)
  const add = () =&gt; {
    // the sum and sum of squares, counted from the scale minimum, identify the mean and SD exactly
    let S = 0, Q = 0
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; k; i++) { S += i * counts[i]; Q += i * i * counts[i] }
    const key = S * 1000 + Q
    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, {key, mean: ssMin + S / n, sd: Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, (Q - S * S / n) / (n - 1))), sets: []})
    groups.get(key).sets.push(counts.slice())
  }
  const fill = (i, left) =&gt; {
    if (i === k - 1) { counts[i] = left; add(); return }
    for (let c = left; c &gt;= 0; c--) { counts[i] = c; fill(i + 1, left - c) }
  }
  fill(0, n)
  return {k, n, l: ssMin, u: ssMax, points: [...groups.values()]}
}

// the chosen mean and SD. it is kept when the scale or sample size changes, and snapped to the nearest possible dot
mutable ssPick = null

// the latest plot's scales and dots, so that a drag that began on an earlier version of it is converted with the current one
ssDrag = ({x: null, y: null, points: null, key: null})























{
  if (!ssSets) return html`&lt;div class="sc-status"&gt;Set the minimum and maximum to whole numbers, with the maximum between 1 and 6 above the minimum.&lt;/div&gt;`
  const {n, l, u, points} = ssSets, R = u - l
  const target = ssPick ?? {mean: l + 0.4 * R, sd: R / 4}
  const chosen = d3.least(points, p =&gt; (p.mean - target.mean) ** 2 + (p.sd - target.sd) ** 2)
  const colour = "#e8710a", fmt = x =&gt; x.toFixed(2)

  const twoCol = width &gt;= 640, gap = 32
  const w = twoCol ? Math.floor((width - gap) / 2) : width, height = 340
  const step = R / 600
  // the uniform-spread bound is the Bhatia-Davis bound scaled down by 1 / sqrt(3), as in the explorer below
  const curve = d3.range(l, u + step / 2, step).map(m =&gt; ({m, floor: sxQuasiFloor(m, n), ceiling: sxCeiling(m, n, l, u),
    uniform: Math.sqrt(n / (n - 1) * Math.max(0, (u - m) * (m - l)) / 3)}))
  const r = points.length &gt; 1000 ? 1.5 : points.length &gt; 300 ? 2 : 2.8

  const plot = Plot.plot({
    width: w, height, marginLeft: 50, marginRight: 15, marginTop: 15, marginBottom: 45,
    x: {label: "Mean", labelAnchor: "center", labelOffset: 36, domain: [l, u]},
    y: {label: "SD", labelAnchor: "center", labelOffset: 40, domain: [0, 1.05 * sxParityMax(l, u, n)], grid: true},
    style: {fontSize: "13px"},
    marks: [
      Plot.areaY(curve, {x: "m", y1: "floor", y2: "ceiling", fill: "#2780e3", fillOpacity: 0.12}),
      Plot.line(curve, {x: "m", y: "ceiling", stroke: "#2780e3", strokeWidth: 1.2}),
      Plot.line(curve, {x: "m", y: "floor", stroke: "#2780e3", strokeWidth: 1.2}),
      Plot.line(curve, {x: "m", y: "uniform", stroke: "currentColor", strokeOpacity: 0.6, strokeDasharray: "1,3", strokeWidth: 1.5}),
      Plot.ruleY([0]),
      Plot.dot(points, {x: "mean", y: "sd", r, fill: "currentColor", fillOpacity: 0.6}),
      Plot.ruleX([chosen.mean], {y1: 0, y2: chosen.sd, stroke: colour, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
      Plot.dot([chosen], {x: "mean", y: "sd", r: 7, fill: colour, stroke: "white", strokeWidth: 1.5})
    ]
  })

  // clicking or dragging in the plot chooses the nearest possible combination, measured on the screen
  Object.assign(ssDrag, {x: plot.scale("x"), y: plot.scale("y"), points, key: chosen.key})
  plot.style.cursor = "crosshair"
  plot.style.touchAction = "none"
  plot.addEventListener("pointerdown", down =&gt; {
    const box = plot.getBoundingClientRect()
    const pick = event =&gt; {
      const px = (event.clientX - box.left) * w / box.width, py = (event.clientY - box.top) * height / box.height
      const p = d3.least(ssDrag.points, d =&gt; (ssDrag.x.apply(d.mean) - px) ** 2 + (ssDrag.y.apply(d.sd) - py) ** 2)
      if (p.key !== ssDrag.key) { ssDrag.key = p.key; mutable ssPick = {mean: p.mean, sd: p.sd} }
    }
    down.preventDefault()
    pick(down)
    let pending = null
    const move = event =&gt; {
      if (pending === null) requestAnimationFrame(() =&gt; { pick(pending); pending = null })
      pending = event
    }
    const up = () =&gt; {
      window.removeEventListener("pointermove", move)
      window.removeEventListener("pointerup", up)
      window.removeEventListener("pointercancel", up)
    }
    window.addEventListener("pointermove", move)
    window.addEventListener("pointerup", up)
    window.addEventListener("pointercancel", up)
  })

  // the frequencies of one set of scores with the chosen mean and SD, with a button to step through the others
  const sets = chosen.sets, values = d3.range(l, u + 1)
  let index = 0
  const right = html`&lt;div style="width: ${w}px; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`
  const draw = () =&gt; {
    const data = values.map((value, i) =&gt; ({value, count: sets[index][i]}))
    const bars = Plot.plot({
      width: w, height, marginLeft: 50, marginRight: 15, marginTop: 15, marginBottom: 45,
      x: {label: "Response", labelAnchor: "center", labelOffset: 36, domain: [l - 0.5, u + 0.5], ticks: values, tickFormat: "d"},
      y: {label: "Frequency", labelAnchor: "center", labelOffset: 40, domain: [0, n], grid: true, ticks: Math.min(n, 6), tickFormat: "d"},
      style: {fontSize: "13px"},
      marks: [
        Plot.rectY(data, {x1: d =&gt; d.value - 0.42, x2: d =&gt; d.value + 0.42, y: "count", fill: colour, fillOpacity: 0.55}),
        Plot.text(data, {x: "value", y: "count", text: d =&gt; d.count, dy: -8, fontSize: 12}),
        Plot.ruleY([0]),
        // the mean, in the same colour as the chosen point on the left
        Plot.ruleX([chosen.mean], {stroke: colour, strokeWidth: 2.5, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
        Plot.text([chosen.mean], {x: d =&gt; d, frameAnchor: "top", dy: -2, text: d =&gt; `Mean ${fmt(d)}`, fill: colour, fontWeight: 600, fontSize: 12, textAnchor: "start", dx: 5})
      ]
    })
    const button = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-quarto btn-sm" style="margin: 0; white-space: nowrap;"&gt;Show another&lt;/button&gt;`
    button.onclick = () =&gt; { index = (index + 1) % sets.length; draw() }
