Three things stand out. First, the dots fill the band only patchily. A mean and an SD inside the bounds need not be produced by any set of whole-number scores, which is why the bounds are a necessary condition rather than a sufficient one, and why the granularity checks of Chapter 12 add to them. Second, the dots along the top edge of the band are the most spread out samples possible at each mean: choose one, and the scores on the right sit at the two ends of the scale, with at most one person in between. The dots along the bottom edge are the least spread out, with everyone on one response or two neighbouring ones. Increase the sample size, or widen the scale, and the dots crowd together, until they are too many to tell apart. This is why, in practice, the bounds are calculated rather than every possible sample being listed. Third, the mean and SD say a good deal about the shape of the data. Choose a dot above the uniform-spread bound, and the scores that produce it are piled up towards the ends of the scale. Choose one near the floor, and the scores are bunched on neighbouring responses. With a small sample, many dots are produced by only one set of scores, so the mean and SD alone are enough to recover every score.
The next plot drops these restrictions and shows the bounds for any scale, sample size, and mean. The shaded band shows the combinations of mean and SD that are possible. Move the sliders, or click or drag anywhere in the plot, to choose a mean and an SD, and the point shows whether that combination is possible. Choose whether responses are continuous or whole numbers, and whether the score is a single item, or the sum or mean of several items, each rated on the scale. For a score made up of several items, tick Cronbach’s α to add a reported α. Tick Observed minimum and maximum to see how much a reported minimum and maximum sharpen the bounds; these are in the units of the score. The dashed grey curve is the Bhatia–Davis bound, which ignores that people come in whole numbers, and the dotted horizontal line is the largest SD that any sample of this size can have, whatever its mean. The dotted curve is the uniform-spread bound from Section 7.2. It is not a bound on what is possible, but on what is plausible: above it, the scores are more spread out than if they were spread evenly across the whole scale, which requires them to pile up at its ends. This plot shows the bounds only. It treats the mean and SD as exact, does not allow for rounding, and does not check granularity, so it does not ask whether a mean or SD could have come from whole-number data at all, as GRIM and GRIMMER do (Chapter 12). It is for building intuition about the bounds. To check values reported in an article, use the app in Section 14.3, which combines the bounds with the granularity checks.

