Swapping the columns does not let the authors off the hook, even when it makes every estimate sit neatly in the middle of its interval. Readers, reviewers, and meta-analysts usually take reported numbers at face value. A meta-analyst who extracts the estimate and calculates its standard error from the width of the interval, as is common, will use the wrong estimate, the wrong standard error, or both, and nothing in the meta-analysis will reveal the error. An error that would be obvious in a plot of one study can therefore distort the pooled results of many.

14.2 How much each check rules out
With values reported to two decimal places, the possible reports of a mean and SD form a finite grid, so it is possible to count how many of them each check rules out (Hussey et al., 2026). The plot below shows this for a small design, with a narrow scale and a small sample, so that the reports that survive are few enough to see individually. Each point is a pair of a reported mean and a reported SD that passes GRIM and GRIMMER. Pairs that fail either of these are not drawn, because there are far more of them. The orange points pass GRIM and GRIMMER, but fail BRIM or BRIMMER: they are the impossible values that granularity checks alone would let through. The plot extends half a scale point beyond each end of the scale and above the largest SD that any sample can have, to show that GRIM and GRIMMER, which know nothing about the scale, accept many reports there too. The table only counts reports with means within the scale and SDs up to that largest value. The purple crosses pass GRIM, GRIMMER, and BRIMMER, but no sample of whole-number scores can produce them. The blue points pass BRIMMEST: some sample of whole-number scores has exactly these values after rounding. Hover anywhere in the plot to see which checks the reported pair under the pointer passes, including pairs whose mean fails GRIM, which are not drawn. For the blue points, the description includes an example set of scores that produces them. The solid lines are the bounds for whole-number data: the largest and smallest possible SD at each mean. The dotted curve is the uniform-spread bound (Section 13.2): reports above it are possible, but need the scores to pile up at the ends of the scale, so they are less plausible for most measures. The table under the plot counts the reports that each combination of checks leaves standing. Change the scale and the sample size to see how the counts change.

